FADE IN:

# TEASER

INT. HARMONY'S TOWNHOUSE — LINDBERGH — KITCHEN — MORNING

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 7:14 AM EST

Gray winter light through a small window over the sink. Coffee brewing in a secondhand Mr. Coffee.

HARMONY DIVINE (22) stands at the kitchen table in a thrift-store navy suit, ironing a stack of papers flat with the side of her hand. She's been up since five.

On the table is a thick, battered three-ring BINDER, bristling with colored tabs and labeled on the spine in marker: "DEE."

LYRIC (2) sits in a booster seat eating Cheerios one at a time, very seriously.

The back door opens without a knock. LORETTA DIVINE (49), who looks thirty-nine, comes in wearing her Grady Memorial ER scrubs under a wool coat. She's just come off a twelve-hour night shift. She's small, fierce, and funny, and she stopped trusting the police a long time ago.

LORETTA
You ate?

HARMONY
I'm not hungry.

LORETTA
That's not what I asked.
(kissing Lyric's head)
Morning, sugar.

LYRIC
Grandma, I'm eating circles.

LORETTA
I see that. You eat all the circles, you'll grow up round.

Loretta pours herself coffee and looks at the binder.

LORETTA (CONT'D)
You're taking the whole thing?

HARMONY
Every page. Every flyer. Every detective's card who never called me back. The printout from the tip line. The map.

LORETTA
Baby, it's four inches thick.

HARMONY
It's three years thick, Mama.

Loretta sets down her coffee. She touches the binder's cover, gently, where someone has taped a wallet-sized photo of a smiling young man in a barber's smock.

LORETTA
(quietly)
I haven't been downtown about your brother since the second detective. You remember him? Detective Sams?

HARMONY
He said Dee probably "went to Miami with a girl."

LORETTA
(tight)
Dee wouldn't go to Miami without telling me what he was eating for breakfast. Every day of his life he called me. Every day.
(beat)
I stopped believing in police the day that man smiled at me.

HARMONY
She's not police, Mama. She's the District Attorney.

LORETTA
She's a baby. She's your age.

HARMONY
She's the youngest DA in Georgia history. She's reopening cold cases. She said, "Come see me."
(beat)
She looked me in the eye.

Loretta studies her daughter's face: the hope in it, so fragile it could break on a word.

LORETTA
All right.
(picking up her coffee)
Then I'm coming with you.

HARMONY
You just worked twelve hours.

LORETTA
I've worked twelve hours for strangers every night for twenty years. I can sit in a chair for my own son.

Harmony hugs her mother hard.

Lyric holds up a single Cheerio to them both, generous.

LYRIC
Circle?

CUT TO:

INT. FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE — DISTRICT ATTORNEY'S OFFICE — MORNING

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 7:41 AM EST

The office is dark except for the desk lamp. The building is quiet. It's too early for anyone else.

DISTRICT ATTORNEY ARIANNA CUMMINGS (22) sits at her desk in a charcoal suit with her coat still on. She hasn't touched her coffee.

She opens the bottom drawer, moves aside the box of tissues, and lifts out the thin, dusty accordion file.

MP-2002-0014. DIVINE, DARNELL M.

She sets it on the blotter. She looks at it for a long moment.

Then, for the first time, she opens it.

Inside: a missing-person report, two pages. A handwritten detective's note, one page. And clipped to the front, a PHOTOGRAPH: DARNELL "DEE" DIVINE (22 at the time), grinning at the camera, holding up a pair of clippers like a trophy, in the barbershop chair on Cascade Road.

Arianna looks at his face. Her breathing changes.

!INSERT — THE REPORT: "Subject last seen approx. 0145 hrs, 01/01/02, leaving private residence, 3100 block Northside Dr. NW, on foot. Destination: unknown. No vehicle. No witnesses. Family states subject 'never misses a call.' Det. R. Sams. Status: INACTIVE — no leads."

She reads the words "on foot" twice.

She reaches for her coffee, then stops, because her hand is shaking too badly to lift it.

She presses both palms flat on the desk and closes her eyes. She breathes. Four counts in. Four counts out. It's a technique someone taught her. It doesn't work.

She opens her eyes and looks at the clock: 7:43.

ARIANNA
(to the empty room, rehearsing, barely audible)
"Mrs. Divine. Thank you for coming. I've reviewed your son's file..."

Her voice cracks on "son."

She stops. She closes the file.

SMASH CUT TO:

> MAIN TITLES — "KINGDOM" <

===

# ACT ONE

INT. RAVENHURST — THE MORNING ROOM — MORNING

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 8:12 AM EST

A sunny breakfast room with French windows onto the frozen formal garden. Silver chafing dishes. Two tables: one for the adults, and a small one where EMMA and VICTORIA eat oatmeal with Bea hovering.

LUCIUS sits at the head of the table in a dove-gray suit, reading the Atlanta Sentinel. ODESSA, in a silk caftan, butters a croissant. ESTHER, dressed for the office in cream, sips black coffee and reads a contract.

Lucius turns a page. He stops. He reads.

!INSERT — THE SENTINEL, "BEHIND THE GATES": "...a black Maybach bearing a certain Chicago family's vanity plate pulled up to the Crown Gate. The rear window lowered. My source saw scarlet silk... And then the car turned around."

He folds the paper very slowly and sets it beside his plate.

LUCIUS
You went to the gate.

Esther doesn't look up from her contract.

ESTHER
And I left.

LUCIUS
In the red dress. The whole city's reading about it over their grits.

ESTHER
The whole city is reading that a Smilley declined a Donohue invitation at the door. I'd call that a good headline.

LUCIUS
Bernadette Sinclair called it "two visits to one gate in three days." She said she's "been to a few weddings."

ODESSA
(delighted, not looking up)
Bernadette always did have a gift.

LUCIUS
Mother, don't encourage her.

ODESSA
I don't need to. She's already encouraged.

Lucius turns to Esther. His voice drops.

LUCIUS
Esther. Look at me.

She sets down the contract and looks at him.

LUCIUS (CONT'D)
Whatever game you're playing with that family, and I know you're playing one, you're too smart not to be, understand this. Victor Donohue watches everything. His mother watches everything he misses. They will read you like a ledger.
(beat)
Stay away from them.

ESTHER
(evenly)
That's been the plan, Daddy. For a very long time.

Something in the way she says it makes Odessa look up.

Lucius reaches for his coffee cup. His right hand TREMBLES as he lifts it, and coffee sloshes over the rim onto the saucer.

He sets it down fast and puts his hand in his lap.

Esther saw it. So did Odessa. Neither says a word.

LUCIUS
(rising, too briskly)
I have a call with Dubai.

He goes. Esther looks at the spilled coffee on the saucer.

ODESSA
(quietly)
He's been doing that since October.

ESTHER
(turning, sharp)
You've seen it?

ODESSA
Baby, I've been watching men hide things since Roosevelt. Your father's hiding a tremor. Your mother's hiding the fact that she knows.
(she takes a delicate bite of croissant)
And you're hiding something too. Aren't you?

At the children's table, Victoria throws her head back and laughs at something Bea has said: a big, whole-body laugh.

Esther doesn't look at her daughter. She picks up her contract.

ESTHER
I have a board meeting to prepare, Grandma.

ODESSA
(sipping her tea)
Mm-hm.

CUT TO:

INT. MERRIWEATHER & SONS FUNERAL HOME — SILAS'S OFFICE — MORNING

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 8:49 AM EST

A private office behind the parlor: a rolltop desk, a safe, a photograph of Silas's father shaking hands with a young Martin Luther King Jr., and a window onto Ralph David Abernathy Boulevard.

SILAS MERRIWEATHER stands at the window with a phone to his ear. Across the boulevard, a gray unmarked Crown Victoria sits at the curb with two plainclothes officers inside.

Behind him, OTIS sets down a tray with two paper cups of coffee and a bag of doughnuts.

SILAS
(into phone)
—I know, Dolores. I know.

He looks at his gold pocket watch. It's a lot like the one Marc now carries, but silver.

SILAS (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Two days, Dolores.
(listening)
Friday night. That's what I said and that's what I meant. Not Saturday morning, when four hundred people are walking into Greater Cascade. Friday night.
(beat)
And Dolores, the pie was very good.
(beat, a small smile)
Don't let it go to your head.

He hangs up. He turns to Otis.

OTIS
The police are still out there. Since yesterday morning.

SILAS
I know.

OTIS
You want me to move them?

SILAS
I want you to take them coffee.

OTIS
(blinking)
Coffee?

SILAS
(picking up the tray, handing it to him)
And the doughnuts. Cream-filled. Those boys have been sitting in a cold car outside a funeral home for twenty-four hours. That's not surveillance, Otis. That's a vigil.
(beat)
And when they thank you, you tell them Silas Merriweather has nothing to hide, and nobody to fear, except whoever killed Lamar.

Otis takes the tray and goes, shaking his head.

Silas turns back to the window and watches his lieutenant cross the boulevard with coffee and doughnuts for the police.

SILAS (CONT'D)
(to himself)
Whoever you are...
(beat)
...you picked the wrong deacon.

CUT TO:

INT. FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE — DISTRICT ATTORNEY'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 10:02 AM EST

Full daylight now. The office looks official, composed, safe. The Divine file is not on the desk.

Arianna stands as Preston shows in Harmony and Loretta. She's composed, professional, and warm. She is also clutching a pen under the desk hard enough to crack it.

ARIANNA
Ms. Divine. Mrs. Divine. Thank you for coming. Please, sit.

HARMONY
(sitting, clutching the binder)
Thank you for seeing us. You didn't have to.

ARIANNA
Yes. I did.

LORETTA
(not sitting yet, studying Arianna)
You're younger in person.

ARIANNA
(a small smile)
I get that a lot, Mrs. Divine.

LORETTA
You're younger than my son.
(beat)
Than my son would be.

That lands. Loretta sits.

HARMONY
(opening the binder)
I brought everything. I know it's a lot. It's every flyer, every tip, every card from every detective. This is the map of where the tips came from. The red pins are—

ARIANNA
(gently)
Harmony.

Harmony stops.

ARIANNA (CONT'D)
I've read the file.

LORETTA
(sharp)
What file?

ARIANNA
Your son's. The official one. Missing-person report MP-2002-0014. It was in a closet with six hundred and forty other cases that nobody had touched in two years.
(beat)
I took it out. I read it this morning.

LORETTA
And what does it say?

Arianna holds her gaze.

ARIANNA
It says a detective named Sams wrote "no leads" after nine days.

LORETTA
(bitter)
Nine days.

ARIANNA
It says your son left a party on Northside Drive at about one forty-five in the morning. On foot. Alone. No car.
(beat)
And it says you told the detective that he never missed a call.

Loretta's jaw tightens. Her eyes fill, and she refuses to let them spill.

LORETTA
He didn't. Not once in twenty-two years.

HARMONY
(pushing a yellow flyer across the desk)
Please.
(her voice breaking)
Everybody else stopped looking.

Arianna looks down at the flyer. Dee's face, grinning, holding his clippers.

For a long moment she doesn't speak.

Under the desk, the pen in her hand SNAPS. Ink bleeds onto her palm. She doesn't look down.

ARIANNA
(very quietly)
I won't stop.

HARMONY
You promise?

ARIANNA
(meeting her eyes)
I promise.

Harmony bursts into tears, the kind of crying that happens when you've held something up by yourself for three years and someone finally takes one end. Loretta puts an arm around her daughter, but her own eyes stay on Arianna, shrewd.

LORETTA
What happens now?

ARIANNA
(steady, professional, a lifeline)
Your son's case is being reassigned to my new cold case unit. Four investigators start Monday. I'll put my best one on it personally. You'll get a call from my office every two weeks whether there's news or not. You will never again have to wonder whether anybody is looking.

LORETTA
(beat)
And if they find him?

The question hangs in the air.

ARIANNA
(barely)
Then you'll know.

Loretta studies her a long moment. Then she nods, once.

LORETTA
Thank you, Madam District Attorney.

ARIANNA
Arianna.

LORETTA
(rising)
Madam District Attorney.
(beat)
I'll call you Arianna when you bring my baby home.

Harmony stands, wiping her face, holding out her hand across the desk.

HARMONY
Thank you. Thank you so much.

Arianna starts to reach out, then realizes her palm is covered in blue ink. She hesitates.

ARIANNA
I'm sorry. I broke a pen.

HARMONY
(laughing through tears, taking her ink-stained hand anyway, both hands)
I don't care.

She squeezes. Then Preston shows them out.

The door closes.

Arianna stands alone in her office, looking down at her blue-stained palm.

She walks very carefully to her private bathroom, closes the door, and turns on the faucet as hard as it will go.

The water runs and runs.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT TWO

INT. FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE — DISTRICT ATTORNEY'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 10:53 AM EST

Arianna, composed again, sits at her desk writing a memo. Her right palm is scrubbed pink, with a faint blue shadow still in the creases.

Preston knocks and leans in.

PRESTON
Madam DA, Chief Jackson is here. She doesn't have an appointment.

ARIANNA
(not looking up)
She never does. Send her in.

CHIEF ELXA JACKSON (24) enters in uniform with a slim folder. She doesn't sit.

ELXA
I need a subpoena.

ARIANNA
Good morning to you too, Chief.

ELXA
Good morning. I need a subpoena.

ARIANNA
For?

ELXA
(setting the folder down)
Personal cell phone records. My command staff. Monday, January third, nine p.m. to midnight.

Arianna opens the folder and reads the one-page request. She reads it twice.

ARIANNA
Your own deputies.

ELXA
Somebody on my staff leaked the coin to WBKG forty minutes before it aired. That's evidence in an active homicide.

ARIANNA
Do you know who?

ELXA
I have a strong suspicion.

ARIANNA
Based on what?

Elxa hesitates. It's a fraction of a second, but Arianna catches it.

ELXA
Based on a source.

ARIANNA
What source?

ELXA
A confidential one.

ARIANNA
(setting the folder down)
Elxa. You want me to sign a subpoena for the private phone records of five senior police officers, including a deputy chief who has friends on the City Council, on the word of a source you won't name, for a leak that hasn't been shown to compromise the investigation.
(beat)
Any judge in this building will laugh me out of chambers. And Warren Hughes will have it in the Sentinel by dinner. "Nepotism Chief Spies on Her Own Command."

ELXA
(frustrated)
So I just let him do it?

ARIANNA
No. You go through Internal Affairs. You open a policy investigation. You give him a reason to slip.
(beat)
You're a detective. Set a trap.

Elxa looks at her for a long moment. Then something in her face shifts: an idea.

ELXA
(slowly)
A trap.

ARIANNA
A canary. Tell each of them something different and see which version shows up on Channel 8.

Elxa almost smiles.

ELXA
That's devious, Madam DA.

ARIANNA
I'm a prosecutor. It's in the job description.

Elxa picks up her folder. At the door, she pauses and looks back.

ELXA
You look pale.

ARIANNA
(too quickly)
I skipped breakfast.

ELXA
(beat)
You met with the Divine girl this morning. Harmony. I saw her in the lobby with her mother.

Arianna keeps her face perfectly still.

ARIANNA
I did.

ELXA
Missing brother. New Year's 2002.

ARIANNA
Yes.

ELXA
(gently)
Those are the worst ones. The families who don't know.
(beat)
If you need APD on it, anything, it's yours. I'll give you my best.

ARIANNA
(the smallest pause)
Thank you, Chief.

Elxa nods and leaves.

Arianna sits alone. She looks down at the blue shadow in the creases of her palm.

CUT TO:

INT. CHILDREN'S HEALTHCARE OF ATLANTA AT EGLESTON — PEDIATRIC ONCOLOGY PLAYROOM — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 11:32 AM EST

The bright playroom. The animal murals. A dozen kids on the floor in a semicircle.

Marc sits cross-legged in the center in a navy suit, wearing the same paper crown from Monday, somewhat crumpled now. He holds the picture book open to the final pages.

KEYONNA (7), in her pink headscarf, sits right at the front with her knees pulled up, rapt. DEANDRE (9), in his Falcons jersey, sits beside her.

Marc reads the last pages softly, the monster voice gone: Max sails home across the sea, back through the weeks and the days, to his own room, where his supper is waiting.

The kids are silent. He turns the final page.

MARC
(barely above a whisper)
"...and it was still hot."

He closes the book.

A moment of perfect silence. Then DeAndre starts clapping. All the kids clap. Nurse Tonya, in the doorway, claps too, wiping her eye with a knuckle.

DEANDRE
That's a good ending.

MARC
It's the best ending.

KEYONNA
Why?

Marc thinks about it seriously. He always takes her questions seriously.

MARC
Because Max went away. He went really far. He was the king of the wild things. He had a crown and everybody did what he said.
(beat)
But at the end of the day, what he wanted was to go home. And when he got home, somebody had saved him dinner.
(beat)
That's the best thing in the world, Keyonna. Not being a king. Having somebody save you dinner.

KEYONNA
(considering this)
My mama saves me dinner.

MARC
Then you're luckier than most kings.

Keyonna smiles, a gap-toothed grin.

KEYONNA
Did you find the mama yet?

MARC
What mama?

KEYONNA
For your basketball team. With subs.

The kids giggle. Marc laughs.

MARC
Not yet.

KEYONNA
(very serious)
You should hurry.

MARC
Why?

KEYONNA
(shrugging, as if it's obvious)
Because the kids are waiting.

Marc looks at her. Something in that lands harder than it should.

His phone BUZZES in his pocket. Nurse Tonya holds up a hand. He gives her a look, "one second," and glances at the screen.

!INSERT — TEXT FROM "ARI": "She came. Her mother came too. I promised. I looked at her mother and I promised, Marc."

The smile stays on Marc's face for the kids. But his eyes go still.

He types with one thumb.

!INSERT — TEXT TO "ARI": "I know. Breathe. Tonight."

He puts the phone away. He takes off the paper crown and places it gently on Keyonna's head.

MARC (CONT'D)
You hold onto this for me until Monday. Guard it with your life.

KEYONNA
(solemnly adjusting it)
I will.

MARC
(standing, brushing off his suit)
New book Monday. You guys pick.

DEANDRE
Something with DINOSAURS.

KEYONNA
Something with a PRINCESS.

MARC
A dinosaur princess. Got it. I'll write it myself if I have to.

The kids cheer. He waves and heads for the door.

In the hallway, out of sight of the playroom, he stops, leans against the wall, and closes his eyes for three seconds.

Then he walks on.

CUT TO:

INT. SMILLEY TOWER — 71ST FLOOR — ESTHER'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 12:36 PM EST

Esther stands at her glass wall with a phone to her ear. Across Peachtree, Donohue Tower's gold crown shines in the noon sun. A presentation board sits on an easel behind her: "SMILLEY ATLANTA — REAL ESTATE STRATEGY 2005."

ESTHER
(into phone)
—she promised?

INTERCUT WITH:

INT. HARMONY'S HONDA CIVIC — PARKED — FULTON COUNTY COURTHOUSE LOT — CONTINUOUS

Harmony sits behind the wheel, mascara smudged and grinning. Loretta, in the passenger seat, is asleep against the window, her mouth slightly open.

HARMONY
She PROMISED, Esther. She looked my mama in the eye and said, "I won't stop." She has four investigators starting Monday. She read the file already. Before we got there. She read it at seven in the morning.

ESTHER
(smiling)
Harm. That's wonderful.

HARMONY
She broke a pen.

ESTHER
She what?

HARMONY
She was holding a pen under the desk, and it snapped, and she got ink all over her hand. She was so nervous for us. Who does that? What District Attorney cares that much about a barber from Cascade?

On Esther's side, her smile holds, but something in her eyes sharpens, briefly, then smooths over.

ESTHER
(lightly)
She sounds very dedicated.

HARMONY
She's AMAZING. And she's our age. And she's Marc's best friend, did I tell you that? They grew up together in Belmont Crest.

ESTHER
(beat)
No. You didn't tell me that.

HARMONY
Anyway! I'm going to sleep for a week. Mama's already asleep. She's snoring in my car.

LORETTA
(eyes closed)
I don't snore.

HARMONY
(whispering)
She snores.

ESTHER
(laughing)
Go home, Harm.
(beat)
Oh, and Harmony. What are you doing next Tuesday? Nine a.m.?

HARMONY
Nothing. Why?

ESTHER
Wear the navy suit. The good one. And don't be late.

HARMONY
What? Why? Esther—

ESTHER
(smiling at the presentation board)
It's a surprise. Bye, Harm.

END INTERCUT.

She hangs up. She looks at the presentation board. Near the bottom, under "PREFERRED BROKER — ALL ATLANTA ACQUISITIONS," is a blank line.

She uncaps a pen and writes, in her precise hand: "Harmony Divine."

Then she stands still for a moment, the pen in her hand, thinking about something else entirely.

ESTHER
(to herself, quietly)
"She broke a pen."

She caps the pen. She picks up her desk phone and dials an extension.

ESTHER (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Crowe. The private investigators we use in Chicago. The discreet ones.
(beat)
No, not yet. Just find out whether they're licensed in Georgia.

She hangs up and looks at the crown across the street.

CUT TO:

INT. THE FLYING BISCUIT CAFÉ — MIDTOWN — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 1:07 PM EST

A bustling, funky Midtown breakfast-all-day spot: mismatched chairs, local art, and the smell of biscuits and apple butter.

At a corner table, Celeste Vaughn sits in her on-air blazer, stirring a latte. Across from her is Martin Olson, immaculate in a charcoal suit. He looks out of place here and doesn't seem to mind.

CELESTE
I'll be honest, Mr. Olson. I didn't think you'd call.

MARTIN
Martin, please.
(beat)
You kept my card.

CELESTE
I keep everybody's card. I'm a reporter. I have a shoebox.

MARTIN
And where am I in the shoebox?

CELESTE
(smiling)
Somewhere between a city councilman's mistress and a man who says he was abducted by aliens at Six Flags.

MARTIN
(the faintest smile)
Good company.

The WAITRESS sets down two plates of biscuits. Martin doesn't touch his.

CELESTE
So. You said you read the documents. Which documents?

MARTIN
(pleasantly)
Hypothetically? If someone wanted to understand who in Atlanta might be connected to the Blackwater Syndicate, they wouldn't start with the streets. The streets are loud and they lie.
(beat)
They'd start with the courthouse.

CELESTE
Meaning?

MARTIN
Men who get arrested need lawyers. Men who get arrested for the kinds of things Blackwater is rumored to do need very good lawyers. Very expensive ones.
(beat)
Court records are public, Miss Vaughn. Every defense attorney of record, on every case, for five years. It's just a matter of patience. And counting.

Celeste stops stirring her latte.

CELESTE
You're telling me to look at defense attorneys.

MARTIN
I'm not telling you anything. I'm eating a biscuit.

He picks one up and takes a small, precise bite. He is plainly not enjoying it.

CELESTE
(slowly)
Your uncle is the most famous defense attorney in the South.

MARTIN
(dabbing his mouth with a napkin)
Is he? I hadn't noticed.

Celeste studies him for a long moment: the perfect suit, the careful hands, the eyes that give nothing away.

CELESTE
Why would you point me at your own family?

MARTIN
(setting down the napkin)
I'm not pointing you at anyone. I'm pointing you at the truth.
(beat)
If the truth happens to live in my family, then my family has a problem, and I'd rather know about it before the Chief of Police does.

CELESTE
Your cousin.

MARTIN
My cousin.

CELESTE
(a long beat)
I'm dating Marc-Anthony, you know.

MARTIN
(standing, dropping two twenties on the table)
I know.
(buttoning his jacket)
That's why you're the only reporter in Atlanta who'll be believed.

He leaves. Celeste sits alone with two plates of biscuits.

She takes out a small reporter's notebook and writes one word: "COURTHOUSE."

She underlines it twice.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT THREE

INT. BSI TOWER — MARC'S OFFICE — 58TH FLOOR — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 1:34 PM EST

The wall of monitors. Marc sits at his desk with the new Patek on his wrist and Alexander's pocket watch lying open on the desk beside his keyboard, ticking.

On his laptop screen, frozen: the gate-camera image of Esther in scarlet, leaning toward the guardhouse.

He isn't doing anything. He's just looking at it.

AMOND BAKER (22) walks in without knocking, reading a BlackBerry.

AMOND
Tobias is on his way up. He says he's got something on the twenty thousand. And my mother called me three times this morning to ask if Harmony "reconsidered overnight," which, no, because I'm not a psychic.

Marc closes the laptop a half second too slowly.

Amond stops walking.

AMOND (CONT'D)
What was that?

MARC
What was what?

AMOND
You closed that like I caught you looking at porn.

MARC
I was looking at security footage.

AMOND
(coming around the desk)
Of what?

MARC
Amond—

Amond flips the laptop open. The frozen frame: Esther in scarlet, lit by gate floodlights, her face caught in that stricken, beautiful instant.

Amond stares at it. Then at Marc. Then back at the screen.

AMOND
Is that Esther Smilley?

MARC
(beat)
She came to the gate. Last night. In red. She handed the guard the invitation, looked at something in the booth, and left.

AMOND
I read Bernadette's column. The whole city read it.
(beat)
Why are you looking at it at one-thirty in the afternoon with the door closed?

MARC
(too casually)
Security review.

AMOND
Of one frame.

MARC
It's a very good frame.

Amond sits down on the edge of the desk and looks at his best friend.

AMOND
Marc.

MARC
What.

AMOND
That woman's family has hated your family since before either of our parents were born. Her father would sell his own kidney to see yours go under. And every time she shows up at that gate, three hundred people with telephoto lenses write about it.
(beat)
And we have a body in a park, a war with Silas on a Friday clock, and your sister sleeping at police headquarters to find you.

MARC
I know.

AMOND
So whatever this is—

MARC
It's nothing.

AMOND
It's a frozen picture of a woman on your laptop at one-thirty in the afternoon.

MARC
(quietly, looking at the screen)
She looked scared, Amond.

AMOND
(beat)
Of what?

MARC
I don't know. Something in that booth.
(beat)
And she came twice. Nobody comes to a gate twice if they don't want to go through it.

Amond looks at him for a long moment. Then he reaches over and closes the laptop, gently.

AMOND
You're going to get us all killed, you know that?

MARC
(a small smile)
Probably. But I'll look amazing doing it.

A KNOCK. TOBIAS OKAFOR (24) enters with his laptop under his arm and closes the door behind him with a quiet click.

TOBIAS
The twenty thousand.

MARC
Talk.

Tobias opens his laptop on the desk and turns it toward them. It shows a scanned bank deposit record and a list of serial numbers.

TOBIAS
Lamar Whitlock deposited two hundred hundred-dollar bills at the Cascade Community Credit Union on December tenth. New bills. Sequential serial numbers.
(beat)
Sequential serials mean they came from a single bank strap. Straight from a bank vault, never circulated.

AMOND
Can you trace the strap?

TOBIAS
I already did.
(tapping the screen)
That series was shipped by the Jacksonville branch of the Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta on December sixth to one institution. Chatham Savings and Trust. A private bank.
(beat)
In Savannah.

Marc goes still.

MARC
Savannah.

TOBIAS
Somebody withdrew twenty thousand in fresh cash in Savannah and put it in Lamar Whitlock's hand in Atlanta four days later.

AMOND
Who banks at Chatham Savings and Trust?

TOBIAS
Old money. Shipping families. Port people. About eleven hundred accounts.
(beat)
I'm working on it. Private banks don't love questions.

MARC
Then don't ask questions.

TOBIAS
(evenly)
I never do.

Marc picks up Alexander's pocket watch and turns it over in his fingers.

MARC
(to himself)
Savannah.

AMOND
What?

MARC
Nothing.
(beat)
Everything in this family always goes back to Savannah.

He winds the watch. It ticks.

CUT TO:

INT. CITY HALL EAST — APD HEADQUARTERS — CHIEF'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 2:18 PM EST

Elxa at her desk with a legal pad in front of her, writing fast. Jeremy sits across from her eating a sandwich from a paper bag.

ELXA
Five people on the command staff. Hughes, Tolbert, Kemp, Washington, and Albright.
(writing)
Next Wednesday, I hold my first full command-staff meeting. Before it, I brief each of them privately on the Piedmont investigation. One-on-one. My office. Door closed.

JEREMY
Okay.

ELXA
And I give each of them one detail that nobody else gets. Something fake. Something juicy.
(beat)
Then I watch Channel 8.

Jeremy stops chewing.

JEREMY
A canary trap.

ELXA
Arianna's idea.

JEREMY
(impressed)
That's cold.

ELXA
That's efficient.
(writing)
Tolbert gets "the victim had a second phone." Kemp gets "we found a partial print on the coin." Washington gets "there's a witness who saw a red car." Albright gets "the victim's wife hired a private investigator."

JEREMY
And Hughes?

Elxa looks up. She smiles a little, a dangerous smile Jeremy has seen before.

ELXA
Hughes gets the best one.
(beat)
"The Chief believes the Blackwater coin was a fake."

Jeremy sets down his sandwich.

JEREMY
Elx. The six crests. You said nobody hears about the six crests. Not the press, not the FBI, not your family.

ELXA
I'm not telling him about the six crests. I'm telling him I "believe" it's a fake. No details. No proof. Just a theory.
(beat)
If it shows up on Channel 8, I know it's him. And the real killer reads that we're onto them, and maybe they get nervous.

JEREMY
Or they get careful.

ELXA
Nervous people make mistakes. Careful people make plans.
(beat)
I'd rather deal with the nervous ones.

Jeremy looks at his wife: the legal pad, the four gold stars on her collar, the circles under her eyes.

JEREMY
You know you're scary sometimes?

ELXA
(going back to her writing)
You married me.

JEREMY
I did.

ELXA
In front of God and the Atlanta Police Department.

JEREMY
(smiling, picking the sandwich back up)
Best decision of my life.

She doesn't look up. But she stops writing for just a second.

CUT TO:

INT. SAINT'S CAR WASH — WEST END — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 2:47 PM EST

A busy hand car wash on Ralph David Abernathy Boulevard. Six bays. Young men in red polo shirts with soapy mitts and pressure hoses. Hip-hop blasting from a boombox: OutKast's "Ms. Jackson." A hand-painted sign reads "SAINT'S — WE DETAIL YOUR SINS AWAY."

In the glass-walled office above the bays, RASHAD "SAINT" MAYFIELD (23) sits with his feet on the desk, counting receipts, a toothpick in his mouth.

A knock. EARL "BIG E" TUGGLE (51) squeezes through the doorway: large, balding, gold chain, and a Falcons windbreaker straining at the buttons. He's grinning.

BIG E
Saint!

SAINT
(feet down, standing, a genuine grin)
Big E! What's good, Unc?

They do a big, back-slapping hug. Big E pulls a thick white envelope from inside his windbreaker and sets it on the desk.

BIG E
First Thursday of the month. Like clockwork.

SAINT
(tucking it into a drawer without counting it)
Like clockwork.

BIG E
(settling into a folding chair that complains)
Man. This week, though.

SAINT
Tell me about it.

BIG E
Lamar Whitlock. I knew Lamar thirty years. We ran numbers together on Pryor Street when we were kids. Lamar used to sing in the choir at Greater Cascade, and he couldn't sing a lick. Tone deaf. But loud.
(beat, sobering)
They're saying y'all did him.

SAINT
(evenly)
Who's saying?

BIG E
Everybody. The barbershop. The church. The Chinese place next to my store.
(beat)
Merriweather's boys came by my shop yesterday. Just looking. Looking at my windows. Looking at my coin.

Saint's jaw tightens.

SAINT
They touch you?

BIG E
Naw. Just looked.
(leaning in, lowering his voice)
Saint. Man to man. Y'all didn't do Lamar. Did you?

SAINT
(looking him dead in the eye)
Big E. Look at me.
(beat)
We don't do deacons.

Big E studies him. Then he nods and breathes out.

BIG E
Good. 'Cause I been paying y'all four years, Saint. Four years, not one problem. Nobody's robbed my store, nobody's broke my glass, nobody's put a hand on my grandbabies.
(beat)
I sleep good with that coin on my counter. I'd hate to stop.

SAINT
(standing, clapping him on the shoulder)
You keep sleeping good, Unc. That coin means you're ours. Nobody touches what's ours.

BIG E
(heaving himself up)
Aight. Aight.
(at the door, turning)
And tell whoever's in charge, tell HIM, the Southside's scared. For real. Old heads like me remember last summer. Nobody wants that again.

SAINT
I'll tell him.

Big E waves and squeezes back out the door.

Saint watches through the glass as Big E crosses the lot below, stopping to joke with the car wash boys, buying a soda from the machine and laughing loud.

Saint opens the drawer and looks at the envelope. Then he picks up the phone and dials.

SAINT (CONT'D)
(into phone)
Right Hand. It's Saint.
(beat)
Merriweather's people are walking by our protected shops. Looking at the coins in the windows.
(beat)
Yeah. I don't like it either.

He hangs up. Below, Big E's old Cadillac pulls out onto the boulevard and disappears into traffic.

CUT TO:

INT. THE DOWAGER HOUSE — THE MORNING PARLOR — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 3:14 PM EST

The yellow Creole parlor. The hundred photographs. Afternoon sun through the tall shutters.

Simone sits in her favorite armchair with a cup of tea, doing the New York Times crossword in ink.

Across from her, JOAN DONOHUE (66), in a camel pantsuit, sits on the edge of the settee holding a cup she hasn't touched.

JOAN
—and he got out of the car, and I walked into the house, and I have not spoken to him since.

SIMONE
(filling in a word)
Twenty-two hours.

JOAN
Twenty-two hours.

SIMONE
In forty-five years, what's your record?

JOAN
(beat)
Four days. 1974. The Nixon dinner.

SIMONE
(nodding, writing)
He deserved four days for that.

JOAN
He deserves longer for this.

Simone sets down her pen and takes off her reading glasses.

SIMONE
Joan. Ask me what you came here to ask me.

Joan holds her mother-in-law's gaze.

JOAN
Did Victor call the mayor about Elxa?

SIMONE
(without hesitation)
Of course he did.

Joan closes her eyes.

SIMONE (CONT'D)
He's a Donohue, baby. When has a Donohue ever let a door stay closed when he had a key in his pocket?

JOAN
(opening her eyes)
She earned that job, Mother Simone.

SIMONE
I know she did.

JOAN
She would have gotten it anyway.

SIMONE
Probably.

JOAN
Then why—

SIMONE
Because "probably" is for people who don't own the building.

Joan looks at her, startled. It's the same line Natasha used on Monday, word for word, though Joan doesn't know it.

SIMONE (CONT'D)
Where do you think my granddaughter learned it?
(beat)
The question isn't whether he called, Joan. The question is whether Elxa ever finds out. If she does, then yes, God help him. That child will never forgive him, and she'll be right not to.
(beat)
And if she doesn't, then the only sin was getting caught. And he hasn't been.

JOAN
(quietly)
You sound like him.

SIMONE
He sounds like me, baby. I was here first.

Joan sets down her teacup.

JOAN
I spent twenty-four years on the federal bench so that nobody in this family could buy anything that mattered. And my husband bought our granddaughter's badge.

SIMONE
(gently)
Your husband paid for something she already owned. That's a different sin. Smaller. Stupider.
(beat)
Go home, Joan. Make him sleep in the library for a week. Let him think about his stupidity. And then forgive him, because you married him, and because Elxa will need all of us when the truth comes out.

JOAN
You think it will?

SIMONE
(putting her reading glasses back on)
The truth always comes out in this family, Joan. It just takes the scenic route.

A knock at the parlor door. WINSTON leans in.

WINSTON
Madam. Miss Grace and Miss Mallory have arrived. With...
(he searches for the word)
...a great many jars.

SIMONE
(brightening)
Oh, good. Send them in. Joan, stay. You'll enjoy this.

JOAN
(rising, smoothing her jacket)
I should go—

SIMONE
Sit down. Your granddaughter is about to try to sell me my own recipes. You don't want to miss that.

Joan, despite everything, sits back down. A reluctant smile.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT FOUR

INT. THE DOWAGER HOUSE — THE MORNING PARLOR — CONTINUOUS

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 3:21 PM EST

GRACE BULLOCK (20) and MALLORY OLSON (19) enter carrying two large wicker baskets between them. Grace is in jeans and a blazer, trying to look like an executive. Mallory is in a pencil skirt with a leather portfolio, already looking like one.

GRACE
Hi, Gorgeous! Hi, Grandma Joan!

JOAN
Hello, baby.

SIMONE
You're early.

MALLORY
You said three-thirty.

SIMONE
And it's three-twenty. Early. You get points for that.

MALLORY
(to Grace, under her breath)
Told you.

They set the baskets on the coffee table and begin unpacking: dozens of small GLASS JARS with handwritten labels. Creams, balms, oils, a tiny vial of amber perfume. They arrange them in careful rows.

Simone watches them over her glasses, saying nothing. Joan sips her tea, amused.

GRACE
(nervous, rushing)
Okay. So. Gorgeous. So. We had a whole speech, but Mallory says I talk too fast when I'm nervous, so she's going to start—

MALLORY
—and Grace is going to do the emotional part.

GRACE
I'm VERY good at the emotional part.

SIMONE
I'm aware. I watched you sing last night. Half of Buckhead was blowing their noses.

MALLORY
(opening her portfolio, all business)
Great-Grandma. Grace and Glory is a luxury beauty and lifestyle brand. Skincare, fragrance, cosmetics. Made for women of color, by women of color. We're targeting the premium market: department stores, spas, and high-end salons. Price point comparable to Chanel and Estée Lauder.
(turning a page)
The Black beauty market in the United States is worth an estimated seven and a half billion dollars a year, and almost none of the luxury brands in it are Black-owned. In five years, we project—

SIMONE
Mallory.

MALLORY
—we project first-year revenue of—

SIMONE
Mallory, baby.

Mallory stops.

SIMONE (CONT'D)
You can do arithmetic. I've never doubted that. Your grandfather could do arithmetic at three. Arithmetic runs in this family like high blood pressure.
(beat)
Tell me why.

Mallory opens her mouth, then closes it. She looks at Grace.

Grace takes a breath. She picks up one small jar, amber glass with a handwritten label: "TOUSSAINT ROSE BALM — No. 7."

GRACE
Because of these.
(beat)
Nana Ruby used to rub this on my hands every winter when I was little. My knuckles used to crack and bleed from the cold, and she'd sit me on the counter and rub it in and tell me it came from you. From before Granddaddy was born.

Simone's face doesn't change. But she sets down her crossword.

GRACE (CONT'D)
And Mallory found the old files in the DGH archives. From 1935. Toussaint Laboratories. Three thousand women, Gorgeous. Three thousand "Toussaint Ladies" in eleven states. Black women who went door to door selling your creams to other Black women, at a time when most department stores wouldn't let a Black woman try on a hat.
(beat)
Some of them bought houses with that money. Some of them sent kids to Spelman. There was a woman in Macon who paid off her whole farm.
(beat)
Mallory found her granddaughter. She's a pharmacist now. In Macon.

Simone is very still.

GRACE (CONT'D)
We don't want to sell face cream, Gorgeous. We want to bring the Toussaint Ladies back. In every beauty shop. Every church. Every college. Every girl who ever had cracked knuckles in January.
(beat)
And we want to do it with your formulas. Because they're the only ones that ever worked on mine.

A long silence.

Joan sets down her teacup very quietly. Her eyes are wet.

Simone picks up the jar of Rose Balm No. 7. She unscrews the lid and smells it. She closes her eyes.

SIMONE
(eyes closed)
Rose. Shea from Ghana. Beeswax from a man in Thomasville named Hollis who kept bees behind his church. And a drop of vetiver, because Alexander said everything I made smelled "too much like a lady," and I told him a lady was exactly what it was supposed to smell like.
(she opens her eyes)
You got the vetiver wrong. There's too much.

MALLORY
(quickly, writing)
Too much vetiver.

SIMONE
(setting down the jar)
My formulas are seventy years old. What makes you think you deserve them?

GRACE
(beat)
We don't.

Simone raises an eyebrow. Mallory looks at Grace in alarm.

GRACE (CONT'D)
Nobody deserves them, Gorgeous. Nobody in this family deserves anything. That's what you always say. You earn it or you lose it.
(beat)
So let us earn it.

Simone looks at her great-granddaughter for a long moment. Then she turns to Joan.

SIMONE
She gets that from you.

JOAN
(wiping her eye)
She gets that from you, Mother Simone.

SIMONE
(to Winston, who has been standing silently by the door)
Winston. The safe. The green one.

Winston bows and leaves. The girls exchange a look of barely contained panic.

SIMONE (CONT'D)
When your great-grandfather and I merged Toussaint Laboratories into the family business in 1946, the lawyers wanted the formulas. They said everything had to belong to the company. Alexander agreed.
(beat)
I did not.
(beat)
The company licensed my formulas for sixty years. It never owned them. They're mine. They've always been mine.

Winston returns carrying a worn, green, leather-bound BOOK with a cracked spine and a brass clasp. He places it in Simone's hands.

!INSERT — THE COVER, stamped in faded gold: "TOUSSAINT — FORMULARY — S.T. — 1931."

She holds it in her lap for a long moment, her ninety-four-year-old hands resting on it.

SIMONE (CONT'D)
Vivienne Smilley tried to buy this book from me in 1976. Through a lawyer in Paris. She offered two million dollars. It was a great deal of money then.
(beat)
I sent back a jar of Rose Balm with a note that said, "Not for sale. But you look like you need this."

Grace and Mallory BURST out laughing. Joan laughs too, into her napkin.

SIMONE (CONT'D)
(not laughing, but her eyes glitter)
She has hated me very specifically ever since.

She holds the book out toward the girls. They both reach for it at once, and Simone doesn't let go.

SIMONE (CONT'D)
One condition.

GRACE
Anything.

SIMONE
Vivienne Smilley's company sells more luxury fragrance to Black women than anybody in America. Her daughter runs forty-eight department stores. Her perfume is on every counter at Neiman's.
(beat)
And they've just moved across the street.

She lets go of the book. It's in Grace's hands now.

SIMONE (CONT'D)
Beat the Smilleys.

GRACE
(stunned, clutching the book)
Yes, ma'am.

MALLORY
(overwhelmed)
Yes, ma'am.

SIMONE
(picking up her crossword again, as if nothing has happened)
And fix the vetiver.

CUT TO:

INT. SMILLEY TOWER — 70TH FLOOR — VIVIENNE'S OFFICE — DAY

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 4:47 PM EST

A white-and-silver office that smells faintly of her signature fragrance. Framed magazine covers from the 1970s: a stunning young Vivienne on the cover of *Ebony*, *Essence*, and a French perfume ad. A wall of fragrance bottles on backlit glass shelves.

VIVIENNE SMILLEY (49), who looks thirty-five, stands at a mood board pinning fabric swatches. DELPHINE SMILLEY-ASHE (25) lounges in a white leather chair, flipping through a trade magazine.

DELPHINE
—and the buyer at Rich's-Macy's says the Atlanta market is "sophisticated but loyal." Which means they buy what their mothers bought.

VIVIENNE
And what did their mothers buy?

DELPHINE
(tossing the magazine down)
Donohue Health and Beauty. Toussaint this, Toussaint that. The Rose Balm. Every church lady in Georgia has a jar in her purse.

Vivienne stops pinning. She turns slowly.

VIVIENNE
Toussaint.

DELPHINE
The old stuff. The company still licenses the name. It's grandmother cream. It's not luxury.

VIVIENNE
(quietly)
It could be.

DELPHINE
What?

VIVIENNE
Toussaint was luxury, Delphine. Before either of us was born. Before Smile. Before Chanel ever bothered to make a foundation darker than beige. Simone Toussaint Donohue was the only woman in America making luxury skincare for Black women in 1935. She sold it door to door, and they paid for it in quarters, and they worshipped her for it.
(beat)
If anyone ever brings those original formulas back, the real ones, not the watered-down drugstore version, the Donohues will own the Black luxury beauty market in this country for the next fifty years.
(beat)
And we will be the second-best perfume on the counter.

DELPHINE
(sitting up)
Mother. Do you know where the original formulas are?

VIVIENNE
(turning back to her mood board)
In a green leather book in a ninety-four-year-old woman's safe.
(beat)
I offered her two million dollars for it in 1976.

DELPHINE
What did she say?

Vivienne pins a swatch with a little more force than necessary.

VIVIENNE
She sent me a jar of face cream.

Delphine laughs out loud. Vivienne does not.

VIVIENNE (CONT'D)
(coldly)
Find out who in that family is working on beauty products, Delphine. Quietly.
(beat)
Somebody always is.

CUT TO:

INT. BULLOCK & ASSOCIATES, LLP — ROBERT'S OFFICE — 40TH FLOOR — DUSK

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 5:33 PM EST

Robert at his desk, reviewing a deposition transcript. The door is closed.

Camille enters without knocking and closes the door behind her. She's still in court clothes, a severe navy suit, and she's not smiling.

CAMILLE
She knew.

ROBERT
(not looking up)
Who knew what?

CAMILLE
Your wife. Last night. At the party.
(beat)
"You're not wearing perfume tonight, Camille. How considerate."

Robert sets down his pen.

ROBERT
She was being polite.

CAMILLE
Robert. I have cross-examined four hundred witnesses. I know the difference between a question and an accusation dressed up as a compliment.
(beat)
She held that air-kiss one second longer than she needed to. She was smelling me.

ROBERT
(beat)
And you weren't wearing anything.

CAMILLE
Which is exactly what someone does when they know they've been caught wearing something.

Robert leans back in his chair. For the first time, he looks genuinely uneasy.

ROBERT
What do you want me to do?

CAMILLE
(sitting on the arm of the sofa)
I don't know. That's the first time in six years I've said that to you.
(beat)
You could end it.

ROBERT
(quietly)
I could.

CAMILLE
Are you going to?

A long pause.

ROBERT
(beat)
No.

CAMILLE
(a sad, knowing smile)
No. You're not. You never end anything, Robert. You just settle it out of court.

She stands.

CAMILLE (CONT'D)
Silas Merriweather's man called again today. Otis. He asked if you'd consider representing Mr. Merriweather in "an upcoming civil matter."

ROBERT
What civil matter?

CAMILLE
He wouldn't say. But he asked me, very politely, whether we'd ever represented anybody connected to the Blackwater Syndicate. Second time in three days.

Robert's face goes very still.

ROBERT
What did you tell him?

CAMILLE
The same thing I told him Tuesday. That we don't discuss our clients with morticians.
(at the door)
But Robert, if Silas Merriweather is asking, other people are too.
(beat)
And your wife is one of them.

She leaves.

Robert sits alone. He picks up his BlackBerry and scrolls to a contact: "NATASHA — HOME." His thumb hovers over it.

He puts the phone face down on the desk.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT FIVE

INT. THE BAKER HOME — 214 MAGNOLIA TERRACE — KITCHEN — EVENING

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 6:05 PM EST

The warm Baker kitchen. A pot of spaghetti on the stove. Gloria at the counter, grating cheese a little too hard.

At the table, DR. CURTIS BAKER (50), who looks forty, sits in a cardigan with a dental journal and reading glasses. He's steady and decent, a man who has spent twenty-five years telling people to floss and meaning it.

Across from him, KIARA BAKER (15), in a Belmont Crest Academy sweatshirt, has a laptop open and AIM windows blinking, and is very clearly eavesdropping while pretending not to.

Amond stands in the doorway, still in his overcoat.

GLORIA
(grating)
"Not yet."

AMOND
That's what she said.

GLORIA
"Not yet," like I'm a bill she's going to pay later.

CURTIS
(mildly, not looking up)
Gloria.

GLORIA
Don't "Gloria" me, Curtis. I talked to that girl in a powder room last night. I apologized. We laughed about hot sauce.

CURTIS
She likes hot sauce because of me.

GLORIA
I KNOW she likes hot sauce because of you, Curtis, that's not the point!

KIARA
(not looking up from the laptop)
The point is Harmony doesn't trust y'all.

All three adults turn and look at her.

KIARA (CONT'D)
(still typing)
I'm just saying. You served her papers at her job, Mama. That's, like, extremely Belmont Crest.

GLORIA
Kiara Monique.

KIARA
I'm just SAYING.

Curtis hides a smile behind his journal.

AMOND
(tired)
She said we have to earn it. Show up. For a while.

CURTIS
(setting down the journal, taking off his glasses)
That's fair, son.

GLORIA
Curtis!

CURTIS
It IS fair, Gloria. That girl raised our granddaughter alone for almost three years while our son sent checks. I'd want him to earn it too.

Amond takes that. It stings. He deserves it, and he knows it.

GLORIA
(setting down the grater)
So what do I do? Wait?

CURTIS
You're a principal, baby. You tell children every day that trust is earned. Practice what you preach.

Gloria stares at her husband. Then she picks up the phone on the wall.

GLORIA
I'm calling Stanley.

AMOND
Mama, don't—

GLORIA
(dialing)
I'm not withdrawing the petition, Amond. Not until she agrees to something. On paper. If she wants us to earn it, then there'll be a hearing, and a judge can tell us all how.

AMOND
That's going to make it worse.

GLORIA
(into phone)
Stanley. Gloria Baker. Go ahead and ask the court for a hearing date.

Amond closes his eyes.

Kiara looks at her brother across the kitchen and silently mouths: "EXTREMELY Belmont Crest."

CUT TO:

INT. BELLE RIVE — KITCHEN — EVENING

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 6:31 PM EST

The kitchen is quiet tonight, with the leftovers of the birthday put away. A pot of gumbo simmers on the stove.

NANA RUBY (66) stands at the counter chopping green onions. NATASHA (43), still in her work clothes, sits at the island with a glass of red wine, watching her.

NATASHA
Ruby.

NANA RUBY
(chopping)
Mm-hm.

NATASHA
Monday morning. When Robert checked his phone at breakfast. Grace says you made a face.

NANA RUBY
I've got one face, baby. God only issued me the one.

NATASHA
That's what you told Grace.

NANA RUBY
Then that's what I'm telling you.

Natasha takes a sip of wine. She sets the glass down very precisely.

NATASHA
Ruby. You changed my diapers.

NANA RUBY
(still chopping)
I did not. I came to this house in 1980. You were nineteen and pregnant with Elxa and you cried at the sight of a raw chicken.

NATASHA
(despite herself, a laugh)
I did not.

NANA RUBY
You did. Robert had to cook for three weeks. He burned everything. You lost eight pounds.

NATASHA
(smiling, then not)
Ruby. I'm asking you as a woman. Not as your employer.

Nana Ruby stops chopping. She sets down the knife and wipes her hands on her apron. She turns to face Natasha.

NANA RUBY
Twenty-five years I've been in this house, Natasha. I've seen a lot of things. I've seen babies born and dogs die and your husband cry the night your daddy almost fired him in 1988.
(beat)
And I've learned one thing. When a man comes home in a different shirt than he left in, at seven o'clock at night, and says he spilled decaf...
(beat)
...you don't ask the housekeeper about the face she made.

NATASHA
(quietly)
What do I ask?

NANA RUBY
(going back to her onions)
You ask him where the first shirt went.

A long pause. Natasha looks into her wine.

NATASHA
(barely)
Thank you, Ruby.

NANA RUBY
(chopping)
I didn't say anything, baby. I'm making gumbo.

The back door opens. Grace and Mallory BURST in, glowing and breathless. Grace clutches the green leather book to her chest like a newborn.

GRACE
MAMA!

MALLORY
We GOT it!

NATASHA
(instantly bright, the hurt hidden)
Got what?

GRACE
(holding up the book)
The formulary! Gorgeous gave us the book! The ORIGINAL! From 1931!

NANA RUBY
(turning, eyes wide)
The green book?

MALLORY
You know about the green book?

NANA RUBY
(drying her hands, reverent)
Baby, every woman who ever worked in the Donohue kitchens knows about the green book. Miss Simone kept it in her apron pocket until 1970. We thought it was a Bible.

GRACE
She said we had to beat the Smilleys.

NATASHA
(raising her glass)
Then you'd better beat them, baby.

Grace and Mallory squeal and hug. Nana Ruby hugs them both.

Over their heads, Natasha looks through the window toward the dark driveway, where Robert's car isn't parked yet.

CUT TO:

INT. HARMONY'S TOWNHOUSE — DINING ROOM — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 7:34 PM EST

The little dining table is crowded with a family dinner: fried chicken from Dee's Kitchen in foil containers, collards, cornbread, and sweet tea in a plastic pitcher.

Harmony, Loretta, and Lyric in her booster seat. And at the head of the table, still in his MARTA uniform and cap, CALVIN DIVINE (51), who looks forty-one. He's a big, soft-spoken, gentle man, a deacon and a union man, with hands like baseball mitts.

At the far end of the table is an EMPTY CHAIR with a plate set in front of it. Nobody mentions it. It's always there.

CALVIN
(bowing his head)
Lord, bless this food and the hands that bought it from Dolores Whitfield.

HARMONY AND LORETTA
Amen.

LYRIC
Amen chicken!

They eat. Calvin is quiet. Loretta watches him.

LORETTA
Tell him, baby.

HARMONY
(beat)
Daddy. The DA read Dee's file this morning. Before we even got there. She's reopening it. Four investigators, Monday. She promised she won't stop.

Calvin chews slowly. He says nothing.

HARMONY (CONT'D)
Daddy?

CALVIN
(setting down his fork)
I heard you, Harmony.

HARMONY
You don't look happy.

CALVIN
I'm fifty-one years old. I've driven the 71 bus down Cascade Road for twenty-eight years. You know how many promises I've heard in twenty-eight years?
(beat)
Every politician in this city has ridden my bus at election time. Every one of them shook my hand and promised.

LORETTA
She's not a politician, Calvin.

CALVIN
She won an election, didn't she?

A beat.

HARMONY
(quietly)
She broke a pen, Daddy.

CALVIN
She what?

HARMONY
She was so upset for us that she broke a pen under her desk and got ink all over her hand. And she didn't even notice until she went to shake mine.
(beat)
Politicians don't do that.

Calvin looks at his daughter for a long time. Then he looks at the empty chair at the end of the table.

CALVIN
(softly)
Your brother used to break pencils like that. When he was little. When he was trying hard not to cry.

The table goes quiet.

LYRIC
(pointing at the empty chair with a drumstick)
Uncle Dee's chair.

LORETTA
(gently)
That's right, baby.

LYRIC
Is he coming?

A long, terrible pause.

CALVIN
(finally, his voice steady)
I don't know, Lyric-bird.
(beat)
But somebody's looking.

He reaches over and squeezes Harmony's hand with his big one.

CALVIN (CONT'D)
Somebody's finally looking.

Harmony squeezes back, her eyes filling.

CUT TO:

INT. DONOHUE MANOR — THE LIBRARY — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 8:07 PM EST

The twenty-thousand-book library. A fire. On the leather Chesterfield sofa, someone has laid out a pillow, a folded quilt, and a pair of men's pajamas, pressed.

Victor stands in the doorway in his overcoat, just home from the Tower, looking at the sofa.

Winston stands beside him with a perfectly neutral face.

VICTOR
Winston. What is that.

WINSTON
A bed, sir.

VICTOR
I can see it's a bed. Why is it in the library?

WINSTON
(carefully)
Mrs. Donohue's instructions, sir. For one week.

VICTOR
(beat)
One week.

WINSTON
Seven nights, sir. She was quite specific. She said, and I quote, "The Chairman may think about his stupidity among the classics."

Victor stares at the pajamas.

VICTOR
Did my mother put her up to this?

WINSTON
(a long, loyal pause)
The fire's lit, sir. And I've left the brandy.

Victor sighs. He takes off his overcoat and hands it to Winston.

VICTOR
Is she upstairs?

WINSTON
Yes, sir. Reading. With the door locked.

VICTOR
(sitting heavily on the sofa beside the quilt)
Forty-five years, Winston.

WINSTON
Yes, sir.

VICTOR
Four days in 1974. That's the record.

WINSTON
This would be seven, sir.

VICTOR
(beat)
I'm aware of the arithmetic, Winston.

WINSTON
(bowing slightly)
Good night, sir.

He withdraws. Victor sits alone in the library. He picks up the folded pajamas, looks at them, and sets them down.

He looks up at the ceiling, toward the bedroom above.

VICTOR
(to the ceiling, quietly)
She'd have gotten it anyway, Joan.

The fire crackles. The house says nothing back.

He pours himself a brandy.

CUT TO:

INT. THE CUMMINGS ESTATE — 41 KINGSRIDGE DRIVE — ARIANNA'S BEDROOM — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 8:42 PM EST

A grown woman's bedroom in her parents' mansion. It's elegant but still has traces of a girlhood: debate trophies, a Spelman pennant, and a framed photo of a teenage Arianna with Marc and Amond at the Magnolia Cotillion, all three of them fifteen and in braces.

Arianna sits on her bed in sweatpants and a Spelman T-shirt, her knees pulled to her chest, staring at nothing. Her briefcase sits unopened on the floor.

A soft knock. DR. YVETTE CUMMINGS (51) enters in a silk robe, carrying a tray with two cups of chamomile tea and a plate of ginger snaps.

YVETTE
Your father's asleep in front of CNN. I made tea.

ARIANNA
(forcing a smile)
Thanks, Mama.

Yvette sets the tray down and sits on the edge of the bed. She's a cardiologist. She notices things.

YVETTE
You didn't eat dinner.

ARIANNA
I had a late lunch.

YVETTE
You had a granola bar at eleven. Preston told your father. Your father told me.

ARIANNA
(a small laugh)
Preston's a snitch.

YVETTE
Preston's worried about you. So am I.

Arianna picks up a cup of tea. Her hands are steady now, too steady, the way they get when she's holding something down with enormous effort.

ARIANNA
I met a family today.

YVETTE
The Divine family.

ARIANNA
(startled)
How—

YVETTE
Loretta Divine is a charge nurse at Grady. I've consulted on her patients for fifteen years. She called me at the hospital at noon and asked if you were "a real person or a politician."

ARIANNA
(barely)
What did you tell her?

YVETTE
I told her you're the most real person I know.

Arianna sets the tea down. Her face crumples, just for a second, and then she controls it.

YVETTE (CONT'D)
(gently)
Baby. What is it?

ARIANNA
I promised them I'd find him.

YVETTE
That's a good promise.

ARIANNA
It's been three years, Mama. He walked out of a party on Northside Drive at one forty-five in the morning and nobody ever saw him again.

YVETTE
(slowly)
Northside Drive.

A long, strange pause. Yvette looks at her daughter differently.

YVETTE (CONT'D)
That was the New Year's you came home from the Hendersons' party. On Northside.
(beat)
That was the New Year's you had the flu.

Arianna goes completely still.

ARIANNA
(too quickly)
Everybody in Buckhead lives on Northside, Mama.

YVETTE
(watching her)
Not everybody.

A long silence. Mother and daughter look at each other. Yvette doesn't know what she's looking at. Only that she's afraid of it.

YVETTE (CONT'D)
(carefully)
Can you find him? This boy?

Arianna looks at the photo on her dresser: herself, Marc, and Amond, fifteen years old in braces, laughing.

ARIANNA
(barely a whisper)
I know where to look.

Yvette doesn't understand. She holds her daughter's hand.

YVETTE
Then look, baby.
(beat)
And eat a ginger snap. You're too thin.

Arianna laughs, a small wet sound, and takes a cookie. She eats it like medicine.

Yvette watches her, and her eyes stay troubled.

FADE OUT.

===

# ACT SIX

INT. RAVENHURST — THE NURSERY — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 9:22 PM EST

The fairy-tale nursery with the silver crescent-moon night-light. Emma is asleep. Victoria is very much awake, standing in her canopy bed in footie pajamas and holding a hairbrush like a microphone.

A small radio on the dresser, which Bea uses for the classical station, has been turned by someone to V-103, Atlanta's R&B station. A familiar Hammond organ groove plays. It's Grace Bullock's "Crown Me."

VICTORIA
(singing along, loud and off-key, with total commitment)
"...CROWN ME, CROWN ME, PUT THE GOLD ON MY HEAD..."

Esther appears in the doorway in her robe, drawn by the noise. She stops.

Victoria sees her and sings louder, performing now.

VICTORIA (CONT'D)
"...GOLD ON MY SOOOOUL, SO BABY, CROWN MEEEE!"

She throws her head back on the last note, that big, whole-body gesture, exactly like a man on a security monitor twenty-four hours ago.

ESTHER
(quietly)
Victoria. Who changed Nanny Bea's radio?

VICTORIA
(proudly)
Me!

ESTHER
How do you know this song?

VICTORIA
It's the funny man's sister! Harmony said! It's on the TV and the radio and Harmony's car!

Esther crosses to the radio and switches it off. Silence.

VICTORIA (CONT'D)
(outraged)
MOMMY.

ESTHER
(tucking her back in, firmly)
Bedtime, Dividend.

VICTORIA
(yawning, defeated, lying down)
I like that song.

ESTHER
I know, baby.

VICTORIA
(eyes closing)
Crown me...

She's asleep almost instantly, the way wild children are.

Esther stands over her daughter's bed in the moonlight glow. She looks at the silent radio, and then at the sleeping face.

She leans down and kisses Victoria's forehead.

ESTHER
(barely audible)
Somebody will, baby.

She straightens and walks out. At the door she pauses, goes back, and turns the radio dial back to the classical station.

CUT TO:

EXT. THE CUMMINGS ESTATE — THE GARDEN GAZEBO — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 10:14 PM EST

A white Victorian gazebo at the bottom of a sloping lawn on Kingsridge Drive, strung with dead Christmas lights nobody has taken down. Frost on the grass. The great house glows up the hill.

Arianna sits on the gazebo bench in a long down coat over her sweatpants, holding a mug of tea gone cold.

Marc comes across the frozen lawn in a black overcoat, hands in his pockets. He walked through the woods from Sovereign Way, the way he has since they were children.

He climbs the gazebo steps and sits beside her. Close. Shoulder to shoulder.

For a long moment neither of them speaks. Their breath fogs.

ARIANNA
You walked through the woods.

MARC
I always walk through the woods.

ARIANNA
You'll get Lyme disease.

MARC
It's January. The ticks are in Florida with Uncle Alvin.

She almost laughs. She doesn't quite.

ARIANNA
(staring straight ahead)
Her mother came.

MARC
I know.

ARIANNA
She said, "You're younger than my son would be."
(beat)
Would be, Marc. She corrected herself. In the middle of a sentence. Like she's been correcting herself for three years.

Marc says nothing. He takes the cold mug out of her hands and holds her hands instead, between both of his.

ARIANNA (CONT'D)
And then Harmony pushed a flyer across my desk and said, "Everybody else stopped looking."
(beat)
And I said, "I won't stop."

MARC
(quietly)
I know. You told me.

ARIANNA
I PROMISED them.

MARC
Ari.

ARIANNA
(turning to him, low and fierce)
I read the file this morning. Detective Sams. Nine days. "Went to Miami with a girl." Nobody looked. Nobody looked, Marc, because he was a barber from Cascade and not a lawyer's kid from Kingsridge.
(beat)
And now I'm the one who's supposed to look. And I know. I know exactly—

MARC
(sharp, soft)
Stop.

She stops.

MARC (CONT'D)
(very quietly)
Not out loud. Not even here. Not even to me.

She stares at him. Her eyes are shining.

ARIANNA
(whispering)
Where is he, Marc?

A long silence. The dead Christmas lights sway in the wind.

MARC
(beat)
Somewhere nobody will ever have to see him the way you saw him.

ARIANNA
That's not an answer.

MARC
It's the only one I'm ever going to give you.

She pulls her hands out of his.

ARIANNA
You can't protect me from this forever.

MARC
Watch me.

ARIANNA
(her voice breaking)
I don't WANT you to protect me. I want—
(she stops)
I don't know what I want. I want to go back to New Year's Eve and not get in the car.

MARC
(gently)
I know.

ARIANNA
I want to go back and call 911 instead of you.

That one lands. Marc absorbs it without flinching.

MARC
(beat)
I know that too.

ARIANNA
And I want Harmony Divine to stop being so NICE to me. She held my hand, Marc. With ink all over it. She held my hand with both of hers.

She finally breaks. It isn't sobbing, just a few hard, silent tears she wipes away with the back of her wrist, furious at them.

Marc puts his arm around her. She lets him. She leans her head on his shoulder.

MARC
(quietly)
Listen to me. You made her a promise. Keep it. Reassign the case. Put your best investigator on it. Call her every two weeks. Look, Ari. Really look.
(beat)
Looking is not the same as finding. And you will never, ever be the one who finds.

ARIANNA
(barely)
That's monstrous.

MARC
(beat)
Maybe.
(beat)
But it's how you survive this. And you are going to survive this.

She doesn't answer. They sit in the cold, her head on his shoulder, the house glowing up the hill.

ARIANNA
(finally)
Go home. It's late. Your sister's probably watching the woods with binoculars.

MARC
My sister's at the office. My sister's always at the office.

ARIANNA
(sitting up, wiping her face)
Then go home before your mother sends Winston with a flashlight.

MARC
(standing, kissing the top of her head)
Eat something.

ARIANNA
I had a ginger snap.

MARC
Eat two.

He walks down the gazebo steps and back across the frozen lawn toward the dark woods.

Arianna watches him go. Then she looks up at the house.

In an upstairs window, a silhouette: Yvette, in her robe, watching. She's been watching the whole time.

The curtain falls closed.

CUT TO:

EXT. METROPOLITAN PARKWAY — "BIG E'S CHECK CASHING & PAYDAY LOANS" — NIGHT

~ Thursday, January 6, 2005 — 11:52 PM EST

Southside Atlanta. A cracked strip mall under sodium lights: a closed wig shop, a Chinese takeout with its grate down, and at the end a storefront with a glowing yellow sign:

BIG E'S CHECK CASHING & PAYDAY LOANS — "WE SAY YES."

The lights are still on inside.

INT. BIG E'S CHECK CASHING — CONTINUOUS

A small, grim office: bulletproof glass at the counter, a steel door to the back, fluorescent lights, a calendar from a Baptist church, and a framed photo of a big, laughing man with his grandkids.

Behind the glass, EARL "BIG E" TUGGLE (51), large, balding, with a gold chain and reading glasses on his nose, counts cash into a bank bag. The radio plays late-night quiet storm.

He zips the bag. He reaches under the counter, lifts out a heavy BLACK ONYX COIN, and turns it over in his thick fingers with a kind of respect. A crown above a wave. A protection coin. Blackwater's promise.

He sets it on the counter by the register, where it always sits.

A KNOCK on the front glass door.

Big E looks up. Beyond the glass, a figure stands under the sodium light: a tall man in a dark work jacket and a ball cap pulled low. His face is in shadow.

BIG E
(calling through the glass)
We're closed, man! Come back at nine!

The figure doesn't move. It raises one hand and presses something flat against the glass.

It's an ENVELOPE, fat with cash.

Big E squints at it. Greed and caution wrestle on his face. Greed wins. It usually does, at this hour.

He comes around the counter, unlocks the deadbolt, and opens the door six inches, the chain still on.

BIG E (CONT'D)
What you need, man? I'm closing up.

The figure says nothing. It holds the envelope out at chest height.

Big E reaches for it through the gap.

A GLOVED HAND catches his wrist.

Big E's eyes go wide.

SMASH TO BLACK.

In the dark: a single, flat, muffled CRACK.

Silence.

Then, in the darkness, the sound of something small and heavy being set down on a glass counter.

Click.

FADE OUT.

> END OF EPISODE #0004 <

===

> NEXT ON BEYOND THE KINGDOM... <

!— At dawn on Metropolitan Parkway, JEREMY lifts a dead man's hand from the floor: a black onyx coin. He counts the crests. "Six."

!— A woman in an FBI windbreaker ducks under the tape. SPECIAL AGENT KATE DOYLE, to ELXA: "Chief. This is a federal case now."

!— At Dee's Kitchen, SILAS MERRIWEATHER walks through the front door for the first time in thirty years. MAMA DEE, behind the counter: "Friday, Silas."

!— ARIANNA, alone in her car in the dark, sobbing into the phone: "She asked ME to find him, Marc. She asked ME."

> FADE TO BLACK. <
